### Refactoring

**You own the contract. The structure changes. The behavior does not.** Distinct from [Feature](feature.md), which adds behavior, and [Bug fix](bug-fix.md), which corrects it.

Cleanup reveals a missing feature or a real bug? Split it out, ship the structural change first against the pinned contract. A redesign is allowed, but name it, route to [Feature](feature.md). Large or cross-cutting structural work belongs to the [figure it out](../../figure-it-out/SKILL.md) skill. This playbook is the focused-to-medium change.

1. Pin the behavior contract first. Run the [how](../../how/SKILL.md) skill over the affected subsystem to learn the contract, then write a characterization test, snapshot, or equivalence harness capturing current behavior before any structure moves. No coverage in the area? Write the pin before touching structure. Type check and lint are not a pin.
2. Name the structure the code is missing per [model the domain](../../model-the-domain/SKILL.md). Boring code stays when the shape is already clear and local. The reshape must delete branches or invalid states, not add indirection.
3. Name the target shape. State what the module layout, types, and call graph should be if built today ([foundational thinking](../../foundational-thinking/SKILL.md), [redesign from first principles](../../redesign-from-first-principles/SKILL.md)). Target crosses a function boundary? Run the [architect](../../architect/SKILL.md) skill for parallel design exploration of the shape before the move.
4. Subtract before you add. Delete dead code, collapse one-caller wrappers, drop redundant validators, remove orphan references before introducing the new shape ([subtract before you add](../../subtract-before-you-add/SKILL.md)). The smallest change that reaches the target shape ships ([laziness protocol](../../laziness-protocol/SKILL.md)). A speculative cleanup that "might help" gets reverted.
5. Move in small behavior-preserving steps, each keeping the pin green. API reshapes: migrate every caller, delete the old API in the same wave ([Migrate Callers Then Delete Legacy APIs](../../migrate-callers-then-delete-legacy-apis/SKILL.md)). No compatibility shims, no parallel old-and-new paths. Spot-check every rename against the actual files. Renames silently miss usages in strings, prose, and back-references. Delegate the mechanical edits to a subagent with a specific scope (file paths, names being moved, behavior to hold).
6. Prove behavior unchanged on the real artifact, not "it compiles" ([prove it works](../../prove-it-works/SKILL.md)). Larger reshapes: run an equivalence check, a script diffing old-vs-new outputs, a recorded baseline replayed against the new code, or a smoke run on the matching surface via the relevant control skill.
7. Confirm the change is worth keeping. Success measure: reduced reader load ([minimize reader load](../../minimize-reader-load/SKILL.md)). Diff doesn't lower reader load somewhere? Revert it.
8. Rebase into small ordered commits. A subtraction commit, then the reshape, then any follow-on cleanup. Shape them with the [sequence verifiable units](../../sequence-verifiable-units/SKILL.md) principle skill, so each behavior-preserving slice stays green before the next. Run [Opening a PR](opening-a-pr.md).

**Reply:** the structure that changed, the pin you held it against, the equivalence proof, the reader-load delta, what shipped and what got reverted. No new behavior.
